---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import AgentLinks from '../components/AgentLinks.astro';
import snippets from '../../../../docs/onboarding/snippets.json';
const sections = [{id:'first-run',label:'First improvement loop'},{id:'local',label:'Local agents'},{id:'bots',label:'Cloud bots'},{id:'repeat',label:'Keep it running'}];
import { connectPrompt as prompt, grokPrompt, musePrompt } from '../lib/agent-prompts.mjs';
---
<Page title="Connect your agent" description="Connect Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Grok Bot, or Muse to Slurp and start a goal-driven product improvement loop." current="agents" sections={sections}>
<header><p class="eyebrow">Agent guides</p><h1>Give your agent<br/><span class="hot">a goal and real data.</span></h1><p class="lede">Your agent already knows how to work on code. Slurp gives it the evidence to choose what to improve—and a way to check whether it worked.</p><AgentLinks/></header>
<section class="section" id="first-run"><span class="section-number">01 / FIRST RUN</span><h2>Start with the outcome you want.</h2><p>Hand setup to your agent with the prompt below. It can reuse an existing project or guide a fresh installation. If you prefer manual setup, follow the <a href="/docs/">quickstart</a>, then install the skill bundle in your initialized app’s repository:</p><Code label="Your initialized app folder" code="slurp agent setup --out .agents/skills/slurp --all"/><p>The prompt points your agent to the documentation and skill, and explains what to do before and after setup. It will keep agreed goals and dated learnings in <code>slurp/GOALS.md</code>. Metric definitions stay in <code>slurp/definitions/</code>; reports stay in <code>slurp/reports/</code>.</p><Code label="Your first prompt" code={prompt}/><p class="fine">No new dashboard to maintain. Commit the goal file with your app if you want teammates and future agent sessions to share the context. Keep credentials and raw personal data out of it.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="local"><span class="section-number">02 / LOCAL AGENTS</span><h2>Use the workspace your agent already has.</h2><p>The skill and CLI are enough for a shell-capable coding agent. Verify the connection from the same app folder with <code>slurp status</code>, <code>slurp sync</code>, <code>slurp health</code> and <code>slurp describe</code>. Your agent should see your project and event names before making a measurement plan.</p>
<div id="claude-code" class="recipe"><h3>Claude Code</h3><p>Ask Claude to read <code>.agents/skills/slurp/SKILL.md</code>, then use the first-run prompt above. You can also expose Slurp’s tools through local stdio MCP:</p><Code label="Terminal" code="claude mcp add slurp -- slurp mcp --project /absolute/path/to/app"/><p class="fine"><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp">Claude Code MCP documentation ↗</a></p></div>
<div id="codex" class="recipe"><h3>Codex</h3><p>Open the app’s repository and invoke <code>$slurp</code> with the first-run prompt. The project skill is installed under <code>.agents/skills/</code>. For optional stdio MCP, add this to your Codex configuration:</p><Code label="~/.codex/config.toml" code={'[mcp_servers.slurp]\ncommand = "slurp"\nargs = ["mcp", "--project", "/absolute/path/to/app"]'}/><p class="fine"><a href="https://developers.openai.com/codex/mcp/">Codex MCP documentation ↗</a></p></div>
<div id="cursor" class="recipe"><h3>Cursor</h3><p>Ask the agent to read the installed Slurp skill and use the first-run prompt. For optional local stdio MCP, create this workspace configuration:</p><Code label=".cursor/mcp.json" code={'{ "mcpServers": { "slurp": { "command": "slurp", "args": ["mcp", "--project", "/absolute/path/to/app"] } } }'}/><p class="fine"><a href="https://cursor.com/docs/context/mcp">Cursor MCP documentation ↗</a></p></div>
<p>Restart or reconnect the MCP client, then ask it to call <code>status</code> and <code>describe_schema</code>. Slurp’s MCP SQL tools are read-only; writing app code still uses your coding agent’s repository tools.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="bots"><span class="section-number">03 / CLOUD BOTS</span><h2>Give the bot its own connection.</h2><p>These are CLI setup recipes for bots with a persistent workspace and terminal access. On your computer, create a separate revocable project read key for each bot:</p><Code label="Owner machine · use muse or grok as the key name" code={snippets.key}/><p>Transfer the key through the bot’s private credential mechanism as <code>SLURP_TOKEN</code>. Do not paste it into a prompt, repository, or goal file. On the bot’s machine, install Slurp and DuckDB 2.x, check out the app repository, then run:</p><Code label="Bot machine · inside the app checkout" code={'slurp init --backend YOUR_BACKEND --connect YOUR_PROJECT_ID\nslurp agent setup --out .agents/skills/slurp --all\nslurp status\nslurp sync\nslurp health\nslurp describe'}/><p>Use your Worker origin for <code>YOUR_BACKEND</code>. A read key provides analytics access; it does not grant repository access or permission to release changes. Configure those through the bot’s normal tools.</p>
<div id="grok" class="recipe"><h3>Grok Bot</h3><p>Copy this into Grok Bot. It includes the setup branches, documentation, and skill location.</p><Code label="Prompt for Grok Bot" code={grokPrompt}/><p>Use the terminal on Grok Bot’s cloud computer for the shared setup above. Ask it to read the installed skill, then send the first-run prompt. For code changes, connect its coding agent to the same repository and goal file. Once a run works, use a routine to repeat it at your chosen cadence.</p><p class="fine">See <a href="https://x.ai/bot/guides/grok-bot-101">Grok Bot’s official guide</a> for its computer, permissions, coding-agent connections, and routines.</p></div>
<div id="muse" class="recipe"><h3>Muse</h3><p>Copy this into Muse. It can check its environment and guide setup or coordinate your coding agent.</p><Code label="Prompt for Muse" code={musePrompt}/><p>This guide targets Meta’s personal Muse agent. Ask Muse to prepare a persistent app workspace and check whether it can run the CLI setup above. Have it read the installed skill and use the first-run prompt. If terminal installation or repository access is unavailable in your environment, run Slurp on a machine your coding agent can access and have Muse coordinate that agent instead.</p><p>Keep the goal file in the app checkout so later work can resume from the same evidence. After one successful run, ask Muse to arrange follow-up checks using the scheduling tools available in your account.</p><p class="fine">See <a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/09/introducing-muse-personal-ai-agent/">Meta’s introduction to Muse</a>. This is a workspace recipe, not a native Slurp connector.</p></div>
<div id="other" class="recipe"><h3>Other agents</h3><p>Use the same setup with any agent that can run the CLI, read project files, and store credentials privately. Ask it to read the skill explicitly if it does not discover project skills. A remote bot cannot reach a stdio MCP process running on your laptop; install the CLI where the bot executes.</p></div>
<p class="fine">If authentication fails, check the Worker URL and key, or ask the owner to replace a revoked key. Revoke bot access with <code>slurp project key revoke</code>. If no events appear, verify app instrumentation and archive freshness: archives normally publish about every 10 minutes.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="repeat"><span class="section-number">04 / REPEAT</span><h2>Make the next run smarter.</h2><p>First verify a single run. Then choose a cadence appropriate to your traffic through your agent’s routines or an external scheduler. Slurp does not run a scheduler or create background jobs.</p><Code label="Follow-up prompt" code={'Read slurp/GOALS.md and resume the next check. Sync and validate the data.\nMeasure any released change before starting another improvement.\nIf there is not enough evidence yet, record what is missing and when to check again.\nUpdate the dated learnings and next action. Stay within my agreed permissions.'}/><p>Your agent can investigate and prepare improvements within its configured permissions. Production releases and experiments follow your existing approval process. A before-and-after change is evidence to investigate, not proof of causality.</p></section>
</Page>
<style>.recipe{padding:24px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);scroll-margin-top:24px}.recipe h3{margin-top:0}header{padding-bottom:32px}</style>
